// clog_measurement_validation_extreme_pidon_2node_gas.prm
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// [Claude, 2026-09-18] 2-NODE FALLBACK VARIANT of clog_measurement_validation_extreme_pidon_gas.prm --
// byte-identical except cellsPerBlock<64,20,15> -> <64,20,30>, halving Z resolution so numBlocks
// (768/64, 40/20, 180/30) = (12, 2, 6) = 144 ranks fits 2 nodes on the multinode partition, instead of
// the canonical file's 288 ranks / 4 nodes. Same physical domain <768,40,180>, same pillar geometry,
// same everything else -- use this one when only 2 nodes are available; see
// jobs/run_144_clog_measurement_validation_extreme_pidon_fresh_2node.sbatch. Everything below this
// point is that canonical file's own header, unmodified except cellsPerBlock itself further down.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Thesis item 3 (THESIS_PLAN.md): a deliberately bigger/more-extreme sibling of
// clog_measurement_validation_sanity_clog_pidon_gas.prm -- same physics/geometry family (same
// pillars/gap ratio, same target velocity, PID gains, particle physics), scaled up in domain size and
// debris count to produce a bigger, more visually/numerically obvious clog for clearer contrast
// against the (also-scaled) no-clog reference. NOT a replacement for the small sanity-clog pair above
// -- both are kept; this is the "make it more extreme" follow-up requested once the small pair's tool
// validation and flow-spinup timing were confirmed correct.
//
// EXTREME, PID ENABLED: 36 spheres (3x the small scenario's 12, radius 4, diameter 8 LU -- still
// LARGER than the unchanged 6 LU pier gap) fed in from X=40-100 at the channel centerline (Y=20),
// staggered releases (startInactive+settlingSteps ladder, 250-step gap, first release at t=20000 --
// same post-spinup timing fix as the small sanity-clog pair, not the original t=5000 the small pair
// itself used before that fix) across the same 3 density tiers (0.25/0.5/0.75) and Z variation
// (44/45/46) as the small scenario, simply repeated 3x on the same 12-entry cycle (X-phase period 4 x
// density/Z-phase period 3 = 12, so 3 repeats give 3 fresh copies of every combination, not new manual
// positions -- reduces risk of a transcription error vs. hand-designing 36 new spots). Expect a much
// bigger accumulated pile against the pier faces (not just filling the gap) given 3x the debris mass,
// and correspondingly larger/clearer clogUpstreamElevation/ExcessPressure divergence and
// avgParticleTransportRate collapse than the small scenario.
//
// [Claude, 2026-09-14]: TWO fixes applied here, after TWO failed attempts, both root-caused from
// archived data rather than guessed:
// (1) release zone moved from X=160-220 to X=40-100 (clearance to the pier at X=384 increased from
//     164-224 LU to 284-344 LU) after job 4234536's first attempt aborted at step 42501 with a
//     [RELEASE-OVERLAP] error (a freshly-released sphere at X=220 collided with an existing floater
//     still lingering nearby). This alone was NOT enough: the retry (job 4234802) hit the identical
//     failure mode at step 48501 at the new zone's own downstream-most position (X=100) -- exactly
//     one reuse-interval (4 positions x 1500 steps = 6000 steps) later, proving relocation only
//     delays the problem proportionally rather than fixing it.
// (2) release spacing compressed from 1500 to 200 steps (last release now at t=27000, not t=72500) --
//     the actual root cause: pulling avgParticleTransportRate from the aborted run showed the flow
//     stays at its healthy ~0.0009-0.0014 rate through t=30000, THEN starts declining (down to ~0.0006,
//     ~40% below baseline, by t=42000, alongside clogUpstreamElevation rising to ~51.7-52.0 vs. a
//     confirmed ~49.62 undisturbed baseline). Both prior failures happened well into that
//     already-congested regime (t=42501, t=48501) because releases kept continuing there (out to
//     t=72500 originally) -- a fixed reuse interval that was perfectly safe under free-flowing
//     conditions becomes unsafe once the clog has slowed local advection enough. Finishing all 36
//     releases by t=27000, comfortably before the ~t=30000-32000 slowdown onset, removes the
//     precondition entirely (no release event ever occurs during the congested regime) without
//     reducing the debris load or widening the spawn zone further.
//
// [Claude, 2026-09-16]: the PID-off sibling file (job 4239231, its first ever attempt) hit its OWN
// [RELEASE-OVERLAP] abort at step 21001 under this same 200-step schedule -- PID-off's flow apparently
// gives floaters less lateral drift near the spawn zone than PID-on's, so 800 steps of same-column
// reuse gap (4 positions x 200 steps) wasn't enough clearance there even though it was fine here.
// Fix applied to BOTH files identically, to keep this pair a genuine controlled comparison (same
// debris-injection protocol, PID.enabled the only real difference): widened the per-release gap from
// 200 to 250 steps (last release t=27000 -> t=28750, still safely before the t=30000 congestion
// onset above). This file's own run never actually hit the overlap failure, but is rerun with the
// wider spacing anyway so both cases inject identical debris on an identical schedule.
//
// [Claude, 2026-09-16]: the 250-step fix above was NOT enough -- THIS file's own job 4239288 failed
// with the identical [RELEASE-OVERLAP] mechanism at the very FIRST reuse of a spawn column (step
// 21251, X=60: fresh release at <60,20,46> vs. the first-ever X=60 release from 1000 steps earlier,
// drifted only to <63.69,21.72,51.56> -- penetration -1.11; the PID-off sibling failed the same way
// one column over, step 21501). The ~7.35 LU drift-per-800-steps figure the earlier fix extrapolated
// from was apparently not representative: drift did not scale up with the added margin, so a modest
// widening of the same fixed-interval schedule was never going to be reliable. Fix applied to BOTH
// files identically again: widened the per-release gap 3x further, 250 -> 750 steps (same-column
// reuse gap 1000 -> 3000), pushing the last release from t=28750 to t=46250, and extended `timesteps`
// by the same +17500 steps (97500 -> 115000) to preserve the post-release settling tail exactly as it
// was rather than shrinking it. Still the same staggered round-robin release mechanism (4 X-columns x
// 3 density tiers x 3 Z values), just stretched -- not a redesign of the release geometry itself.
//
// [Claude, 2026-09-16]: BOTH of the above 2026-09-16 fixes were timing-only and both failed (one for
// too little same-column margin, the other -- in the PID-off sibling -- for giving an upstream floater
// too much time to drift downstream into the NEXT column before it fired: two failure modes pulling
// in opposite directions on the same knob). Root cause reframed: release Z was 44-46, i.e. AT/just
// below the water surface (absoluteLiquidHeight = avgParticleDiameter*(liquidHeightFactor+
// floorHeightFactor) = 8.00194*(2.0+4.1393) = 49.13), so a fresh release sits in the same thin layer
// where every already-floating sphere ends up riding, regardless of timing. Computed the worst case
// directly: the most buoyant sphere here (densityRatio=0.25, r=4) has an Archimedes-equilibrium
// submerged cap depth of ~2.68 (solving the spherical-cap volume fraction), giving CoM~50.42 and a
// TOP at ~54.42; allowing ~4 LU of transient overshoot above equilibrium (the d025 box equilibrium
// run this same session overshot its own final value by ~3.15 LU before damping down) puts the
// realistic worst-case existing-floater top around ~58.4. Fix: raised release Z from 44/45/46 to
// 70/71/72 (X, densityRatio, settlingSteps all UNCHANGED) -- 11.6-13.6 LU clear of that 58.4 worst
// case, comfortably above the 8 LU (2*radius) needed for zero overlap regardless of release timing or
// horizontal drift. This changes the injection condition from "already partially submerged at the
// surface" to "dropped from just above it" (a ~21-28 LU fall through the gas region under gravity to
// waterline 49.13) -- arguably more physically realistic for debris entering a channel, and at this
// gravity a gentle low-Mach entry (fall velocity ~0.017 LU/step at the estimated gravity magnitude,
// well below the target flow velocity 0.04). The widened timing from the two attempts above is left
// unchanged as a still-harmless hedge, not because it's still load-bearing for this specific bug.
//
// Domain <768, 40, 90 -> 180 as of 2026-09-18, see below>: X DOUBLED from the small scenario's 384
// (every X-valued position below -- pillars, probes, release zone -- is simply the small scenario's
// own X doubled, which preserves every relative distance/margin exactly: e.g. pillars at the new
// domain's exact midpoint (384, same as the small scenario's pillars sitting at ITS domain's exact
// midpoint 192), so this is a clean geometric scale-up, not a redesign). Y=40 and the pier gap (still
// 6 LU, still < the unchanged 8 LU sphere diameter) are left UNCHANGED -- only X grows, giving 2x the
// queueing/pile-up room without touching the "one sphere physically cannot pass" guarantee the small
// scenario's own geometry relies on.
// periodicity <1,0,0> (X periodic, Y walled), same as every two-pillar scenario in this project.
//
// [Claude, 2026-09-18]: Z raised 90 -> 180 (doubled). The completed run under the old Z=90 (job
// 4242919) showed clogUpstreamElevation pinned at EXACTLY 90.0 -- the domain's own top wall
// (`Border direction T`, NoSlip) -- for ~40% of the run (t>=73900 through the end), never exceeding
// it by even a rounding error. That's a hard physical ceiling of the SIMULATED BOX, not a converged
// equilibrium of the real system being modeled: the box was sized for normal operation's ~35 LU of
// headroom above the ~50-55 baseline free surface, and the extreme clog blew straight through that
// budget. Doubling Z gives ~125 LU of headroom above the same baseline instead -- see THESIS_PLAN.md,
// 2026-09-18, for the derivation and the two-rank-count-variant sbatch scripts this produced.
// BodyBC's own max-Z (below, BoundaryParameters) raised to match -- a pillar must span the full
// domain height or the top half of its column would be open water.
//
// cellsPerBlock <64,20,15> (unchanged) -> numBlocks (768/64, 40/20, 180/15) = (12, 2, 12) = 288 ranks,
// 4 nodes on the multinode partition -- see jobs/run_288_clog_measurement_validation_extreme_pidon_fresh.sbatch.
// A coarser cellsPerBlock<64,20,30> variant (144 ranks / 2 nodes, same physical domain, half the Z
// resolution) exists as clog_measurement_validation_extreme_pidon_2node_gas.prm for when only 2 nodes
// are available -- see jobs/run_144_clog_measurement_validation_extreme_pidon_fresh_2node.sbatch.
//
// Physics (targetMeanVelocityMagnitude, PID gains, Re/Fr/We, avgParticleDiameterOverride/dxSIOverride,
// densityRatio, liquidHeightFactor, floorHeightFactor) are IDENTICAL to the small scenario -- this is a
// pure geometry/debris-count scale-up of the same physical setup, not a different flow regime.
//
// timesteps: 115000 (see the 2026-09-16 note above -- this is the original 97500 + the same +17500
// the last release moved by, so the post-release settling tail is unchanged from before).
//
// [Claude, 2026-09-17]: floater population redesigned per explicit request --
// (1) densityRatio set {0.25,0.5,0.75} -> {0.5,1.0,1.5}: 1.0 is neutrally buoyant (drifts at whatever
//     depth it starts, rises/sinks under no net force); 1.5 is DENSER than water and will genuinely
//     sink to the bed under this project's MEM-only buoyancy convention (gravity applied as full
//     weight, buoyancy supplied entirely by the hydrodynamic MEM force) -- this is a deliberate,
//     physically sensible choice, not an error: it models a mixed floating/submerged debris load
//     (c.f. the Pont Ferroviere case study's own recorded composition of wood + building rubble,
//     Section~sec:pont-ferroviere-showcase, where rubble is denser than water). The release height
//     (Z=70-72, see the 2026-09-16 note above) stays safe either way: it was originally sized against
//     the OLD densityRatio=0.25 tier's own worst-case float height (the most buoyant, hence highest-
//     riding, tier); the new lightest tier (0.5) floats LOWER than the old 0.25 did, so the existing
//     margin only gets larger, not smaller.
// (2) release restructured from one floater at a time to a 2(Y)x5(X)=10-floater simultaneous batch per
//     release event: Y in {14,26} (was the single centreline Y=20 -- spaced 12 apart, i.e. >2*radius=8,
//     so the two rows can never overlap each other), X in {40,60,80,100,120} (was {40,60,80,100} -- one
//     more column, still 20 LU apart, unchanged spacing convention). 10 release events (r=0..9) at
//     settlingSteps = 20000 + r*3000 give the requested 100 floaters total (10/release).
//     densityRatio/Z pairing kept 1:1 as before (now 0.5<->Z=70, 1.0<->Z=71, 1.5<->Z=72), cycled over a
//     FLAT index across all 100 floaters (batch-major, then Y, then X) so each batch mixes all three
//     tiers rather than one tier per batch -- gives a 34/33/33 split (100 doesn't divide evenly by 3,
//     so exact equality is impossible) and matches the original scenario's own intent of a heterogeneous
//     debris mix arriving continuously, not segregated waves of one density at a time.
// (3) batch-to-batch gap set to 3000 steps, NOT the old single-floater 750-step cadence -- IMPORTANT
//     distinction: previously a given (X) spawn column only recurred every 4th release (4 columns
//     cycling), so the true same-spot reuse gap was 4*750=3000 despite the schedule stepping by 750.
//     Now, because every batch uses ALL 10 (X,Y) grid points at once, EVERY spawn point recurs on
//     EVERY SINGLE batch -- so the reuse gap equals the batch gap directly, and using the old 750
//     would reproduce the exact [RELEASE-OVERLAP] crash chased down earlier this session (root-caused
//     to needing >=3000 steps of same-spot reuse margin). 3000 was applied directly rather than
//     re-discovering this the hard way. Last release moved from t=46250 to t=47000 (+750); timesteps
//     extended by the same +750 (115000 -> 115750) to preserve the settling tail exactly as before.
//
// [Claude, 2026-09-17]: pillar geometry redesigned per explicit request -- ONE big central pillar
// instead of two, replacing the old 3-aperture layout (5/6/5 LU gaps either side of a 6-LU centre slit)
// with a symmetric 2-aperture layout (one slit on each side of a single wide pillar). Domain Y=40
// unchanged; single CylinderPillar centred at the domain's own Y-midpoint (Y=20), radius 14 (diameter
// 28 LU) chosen so BOTH remaining slits are 20-14=6 LU wide -- comfortably < the 8 LU sphere diameter,
// so a sphere still cannot physically pass through either side (same "guaranteed full blockage"
// property the old 3-gap layout had, just architecturally simpler: 2 symmetric apertures instead of 3
// uneven ones). X=384/Z=45 (unchanged from the old pillars' own values -- Z is an arbitrary reference
// point along the pillar's own effectively-infinite vertical axis, not a real bound). Release positions
// (X=40-120, far upstream of the pillar at X=384) are unaffected by this Y-axis change.
//
// ClogMeasurement probes restructured to match: "one probe pair before and after EACH slit" (explicit
// request), replacing the old gap-centreline pair (Y=20, now INSIDE the new pillar's own solid body)
// and pillar-face pair (Y=11). Both existing probe-pair slots (upstreamPos/downstreamPos and
// pillarUpstreamPos/pillarDownstreamPos -- SimulationParams.h supports exactly these two, no more) are
// repurposed rather than requiring a code change: the first pair now sits at the LEFT slit's own
// centreline (Y=3, i.e. the midpoint of the [0,6] opening), the second at the RIGHT slit's centreline
// (Y=37, midpoint of the [34,40] opening). X unchanged (280/500, the same 52/58 LU offset from the
// pillar centre as before). probeHalfWidthXY=2 gives each probe a +-2 LU window (Y in [1,5] and
// [35,39] respectively), staying safely inside its own 6-LU slit with a full 1 LU clearance from both
// the wall and the pillar face on either side. Two independent slit measurements is if anything a
// BETTER match for \citeauthor{schalko_impact_2023}'s own finding motivating this two-probe design in
// the first place (real lateral non-uniformity across a partial-width jam) than the old asymmetric
// gap-centre/pillar-face pairing was.
// [Claude, 2026-09-17]: user requested 60%/40% pillar/open split instead of the 70/30 above, but
// exact 60/40 (pillar radius 12, each slit 8 LU) puts the slit EXACTLY equal to the sphere's own
// diameter (8 LU) -- zero real clearance, not a safe guarantee. Rather than settle for a slightly
// looser split, made the spheres bigger instead (explicit request): radius 4 -> 5 (diameter 8 -> 10),
// restoring a real 2 LU margin at the now-8-LU slit while keeping the requested 60/40 split exactly.
// This bigger sphere ripples into every other size-dependent margin computed above, all re-derived
// (not just scaled by feel):
//  - pillar radius 14 -> 12 (60% pillar / 40% open, 8 LU per slit, i.e. 20% each side).
//  - release Y-row spacing 12 -> 14 (Y in {13,27}, was {14,26}) to keep the same 4 LU clearance
//    margin over the new 2*radius=10 minimum, rather than letting the margin silently shrink.
//  - release Z bumped {70,71,72} -> {73,74,75}: recomputed the worst-case existing-floater top for
//    the new lightest tier (0.5, exactly half-submerged, so by symmetry its centre sits AT the
//    waterline 49.13) with the new radius 5: top=54.13, +4 LU overshoot buffer=58.13 worst case
//    (incidentally close to the old 58.4 despite the bigger sphere, since the new lightest tier 0.5
//    rides lower than the old lightest tier 0.25 did) -- new release Z gives 14.9-16.9 LU clearance
//    against a 10 LU need, i.e. a LARGER margin than before (4.9-6.9 LU vs the old ~3.6 LU).
//  - probe Y repositioned to the new slit centrelines: left slit [0,8] -> Y=4; right slit [32,40] ->
//    Y=36 (both probeHalfWidthXY=2 windows now sit with 2 LU clearance from wall/pillar either way).
//  - batch reuse GAP 3000 -> 4000: the required drift-to-clear distance scales with sphere diameter
//    (was 8, now 10, a 25% increase); naive linear scaling of the empirically-validated 3000 gives
//    3750, rounded up to 4000 for margin rather than cutting it as close as the diameter-8 case was.
//    Last release moves 47000 -> 56000 (+9000 over 9 gaps); timesteps extended by the same tail
//    (68750) to 124750; VTK writeFrequency updated to 124750/5=24950 to match.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

BlockForestParameters
{
   cellsPerBlock                 < 64, 20, 30 >;
   periodicity                   < 1, 0, 0 >;
   loadSnapshot                  false;
   storeSnapshot                 true;
   snapshotFrequency             5000;
   snapshotBaseFolder            snapshots;
}

DomainParameters
{
   domainSize         <768, 40, 180>;
   wavePeriods        1;
   liquidHeightFactor 2.0;
   floorHeightFactor  4.1393;
   initialAmplitude   0;
}

PIDParameters
{
   targetMeanVelocityMagnitude 0.04;
   proportionalGain            2e-4;
   derivativeGain              1e-6;
   integralGain                2e-4;
   maxRamp                     1e-4;
   minActuatingVariable        0;
   maxActuatingVariable        1e-3;
   enabled                     true;
}

PhysicsParameters
{
   enableWetting     false;
   timesteps         124750;
   Re                3100;
   Fr                1.31;
   We                15.6188;
   useHardcodedUnits false;
   // no gravityOverride -- dynamically computed, see file header
}

ParticleParameters
{
   inFileName spheres_out.dat;   // never opened -- see avgParticleDiameterOverride below
   bedCopiesInX 1;
   bedCopiesInY 1;
   avgParticleDiameterOverride 8.001943372119138;
   dxSIOverride                0.000362694127490625;
   densityRatio 2.55;
   fixingHeightFactor 1.5;
   frictionCoefficient 0.5;
   restitutionCoefficient 0.5;
   numSubCycles 10;
   useLubricationCorrection true;
   useNoSlipParticles false;
   useParticleBed false;
}

CylinderPillars
{
   // [Claude, 2026-09-17]: single big central pillar replacing the old two -- see file header for the
   // full geometry derivation (radius 12, i.e. 60% of the Y=40 domain, leaving an 8 LU slit on each
   // side, both < the 10 LU sphere diameter).
   CylinderPillar { center < 384, 20, 45 >; radius 12; }
}
